        {
          "type": "practice",
          "title": "Argue Like Lady Philosophy",
          "content": "Choose a real or hypothetical source of unhappiness (loss of a job, a relationship ending, a public failure). Now write Lady Philosophy's response using Boethius's actual method:\n\n1. **Diagnose the misattributed cause.** What is the person blaming (bad luck, another person, fate) that is really just the changeable nature of external goods?\n2. **Run the elimination argument.** Was the lost thing (status, money, approval) ever the kind of thing that could ground stable happiness? Why or why not?\n3. **Point toward the unchanging alternative.** What, in Boethius's framework, would actually qualify as a secure foundation for happiness?\n\nKeep your answer in the voice of a confident, slightly stern teacher — that's Lady Philosophy's register throughout the text.",
          "visual": {
            "type": "diagram",
            "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"65\" font-size=\"34\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Practice: Be Philosophy</text><rect x=\"130\" y=\"180\" width=\"840\" height=\"130\" rx=\"18\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.12)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"240\" font-size=\"21\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1. Diagnose the misattributed cause</text><rect x=\"130\" y=\"340\" width=\"840\" height=\"130\" rx=\"18\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.12)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"400\" font-size=\"21\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2. Run the elimination argument</text><rect x=\"130\" y=\"500\" width=\"840\" height=\"130\" rx=\"18\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.12)\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"560\" font-size=\"21\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">3. Point to the unchanging alternative</text></svg>",
            "caption": "Structured exercise: write a Boethian consolation for a real loss."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "title": "Transmitting Aristotle's Logic",
          "content": "Beyond the *Consolation*, Boethius has an equally important historical role: he set out, ambitiously, to translate the entire works of both **Plato** and **Aristotle** into Latin and reconcile them — a project cut short by his execution. He completed translations and commentaries on Aristotle's logical works, particularly the *Categories* and *On Interpretation*, along with Porphyry's *Isagoge* (an introduction to Aristotelian logic).\n\nThis mattered enormously for what came next. As direct knowledge of Greek receded in the early medieval Latin West, Boethius's translations and commentaries became **nearly the only access** later scholars had to Aristotelian logic for roughly five centuries — until the 12th-century translation wave (Lesson 6) recovered much more of Aristotle's corpus, largely through Arabic intermediaries. Boethius is, in a very literal sense, the bridge that kept the tools of formal logic alive in Latin Christendom through the intellectual contraction of the early Middle Ages, setting the stage for the scholastic method that follows.",
          "visual": {
            "type": "diagram",
            "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"65\" font-size=\"34\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Boethius the Translator</text><rect x=\"120\" y=\"180\" width=\"860\" height=\"150\" rx=\"18\" fill=\"rgba(59,130,246,0.12)\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"240\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Translates Aristotle's Categories,</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"285\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">On Interpretation, Porphyry's Isagoge</text><rect x=\"120\" y=\"360\" width=\"860\" height=\"150\" rx=\"18\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.15)\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"420\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffd700\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Nearly the only Latin access</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"465\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">to Aristotelian logic for ~500 years</text><rect x=\"120\" y=\"540\" width=\"860\" height=\"140\" rx=\"18\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.12)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"600\" font-size=\"21\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Bridge to Scholasticism</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"640\" font-size=\"19\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Lesson 6</text></svg>",
            "caption": "Boethius's logical translations kept Aristotle alive in the Latin West."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "takeaway",
          "title": "A Widely Read Book",
          "content": "Few philosophical texts have had a longer continuous readership than the *Consolation*. It was reportedly translated into **Old English** under (or associated with) King **Alfred the Great** in the 9th century; into **Middle English** by **Geoffrey Chaucer** in the 14th century (his *Boece*); and it was read, copied, and commented on continuously across a millennium of European intellectual life, shaping everything from Dante to Elizabeth I (who also produced a translation).\n\nBoethius's genuine achievement is holding together two things that don't always coexist: rigorous, technical philosophical argument, and a work of real literary and emotional power, written under the most extreme circumstances an author can face. His transmission of Aristotelian logic, meanwhile, quietly prepares the ground for the next lesson's subject — the rise of a systematic, logic-driven method for doing philosophy and theology together.",
          "visual": {
            "type": "diagram",
            "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"65\" font-size=\"34\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">A Thousand-Year Bestseller</text><rect x=\"120\" y=\"180\" width=\"270\" height=\"180\" rx=\"16\" fill=\"rgba(59,130,246,0.12)\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"255\" y=\"240\" font-size=\"19\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Old English</text><text x=\"255\" y=\"280\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">King Alfred</text><text x=\"255\" y=\"310\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">9th century</text><rect x=\"415\" y=\"180\" width=\"270\" height=\"180\" rx=\"16\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.12)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"240\" font-size=\"19\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Middle English</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"280\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Chaucer's Boece</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"310\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">14th century</text><rect x=\"710\" y=\"180\" width=\"270\" height=\"180\" rx=\"16\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.12)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"845\" y=\"240\" font-size=\"19\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Elizabeth I</text><text x=\"845\" y=\"280\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">own translation</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"480\" font-size=\"21\" fill=\"#ffd700\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Read continuously for a thousand years</text></svg>",
            "caption": "The Consolation's remarkable millennium-long readership across Europe."
          }
        }
